ser has F2, F3, F4, which are honor tiles. So All Simples (no honors) is not possible. So maybe they are going for a different pattern. Let's think about the possible options.\\n\\nIf they take Chi W6, they can form a Chow of 4-5-6. Then their hand would have that, plus the existing B4-B5-B6. But since they have different suits, maybe they are building towards a Mixed Straight? Mixed Straight requires chows in different suits for 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. But the user's existing B4-B5-B6 is a 4-5-6 in Dots. If they take a 4-5-6 in Characters (W), then they have two 4-5-6 in different suits. But Mixed Straight needs one of each suit. So maybe not helpful.\\n\\nAlternatively, if they take Chi W7, forming 5-6-7 in W. Then they have B4-B5-B6 (Dots) and W5-6-7. Not sure.\\n\\nAlternatively, if they take Chi W8, forming 6-7-8 in W. Then they have that, plus B4-B5-B6. Still not a pattern.\\n\\nWait, maybe they are going for All Chows. All Chows is a pattern where all melds are chows and the pair is suited. The user's existing meld is a chow (B4-B5-B6). If they take another chow in W, then they have two chows, and need two more chows and a pair. But their hand has W4-W9. Let's see: if they take 4-5-6 (W), then they have W4, W5, W6. Then they can have 7-8-9 as another chow. They have W7, W8, W9 (two). So if they have two W9s, one can be part of the 7-8-9 chow, and the other is a pair. Then they would have two chows and a pair. Plus the existing B4-B5-B6. But that would be three chows (B4-5-6, W4-5-6, W7-8-9) and a pair. That would make four chows and a pair, which would satisfy All Chows (2 points). But since they have two suits, it's not a Full Flush. But All Chows is a 2-point pattern. \\n\\nSo taking Chi W6 would allow them to form a chow in W, then maybe later form another chow. But they need to see if they can complete the hand. However, the current valid actions are only for the immediate Chi. So the user has to decide whether to take one of the Chi options now.\\n\\nAltern